      <h2 style="margin-top:34px">
        What a tier is
      </h2>
      <p>
        Your wallet&#39;s $RECEIPT balance is valued
        <b>        in dollars
</b>
        . That dollar figure puts you on a rung. The higher the rung, the more of each receipt comes back and the more you can earn in a day. That is the whole idea — there is nothing to activate and no code to enter.
      </p>
      <div class="ladder" id="ladder">
      </div>
      <div class="band ok" style="grid-template-columns:1fr;margin:22px 0">
        <p>
          <b>          Staking pays more, twice over.
</b>
          Stake $RECEIPT for 3, 7 or 30 days and every receipt pays
          <b>          2% on top of your tier&#39;s rate
</b>
          with
          <b>          a quarter more room in the day
</b>
          — Gold goes from 3% and $25 a day to 5% and $31.25. What you stake also keeps counting toward your tier at the price you staked at, even if the price falls.
          <a href="/lock">          How staking works →
</a>
        </p>
      </div>
      <h2>
        How the rate and the day work
      </h2>
      <p>
        Two numbers decide everything: the percentage your tier pays on a receipt, and the most it can pay you in a day.
      </p>
      <div class="sheet">
        <div class="k">
          Worked example
        </div>
        <div class="row">
          <span class="m">          Costco receipt
</span>
          <b id="ex-total">          $50.00
</b>
        </div>
        <div class="row">
          <span class="m">          Your tier&#39;s rate
</span>
          <b id="ex-cap">          3%
</b>
        </div>
        <div class="rule">
        </div>
        <div class="row">
          <span class="m">          That much of the receipt
</span>
          <b id="ex-match">          $1.50
</b>
        </div>
        <div class="back">
          <span>          YOU GET BACK
</span>
          <b id="ex-back">          $1.50 in COST
</b>
        </div>
        <p class="note">
          Paid in the stock of the shop you bought from, not in cash.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p>
        <b>        The rate is the whole rule.
</b>
        A receipt pays your tier&#39;s percentage of its total — 3% of a $50 shop is $1.50 — and that is all there is to it. Silver pays 2% into a $5 day, Gold 3% into a $25 day; every rung up is worth more than the one below it, in the rate and in the day.
      </p>
      <p>
        The second number is the
        <b>        daily cap
</b>
        : the most one wallet can be paid across a rolling 24 hours, however many receipts it scans. It rises as you move up the ladder, and it resets every day.
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>        Staking adds two points and a quarter of a day.
</b>
        $RECEIPT staked for a term pays 2 percentage points more on every receipt and lifts the daily cap by 25%, at every tier, for as long as the term runs. At Silver that is 4% instead of 2%, into a $6.25 day.
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>        Holding nothing earns nothing.
</b>
        A scan from a wallet under $100 of $RECEIPT is still read and checked, but it pays nothing. The tokens are what fund the rewards, so holding them is what starts the earning.
      </p>
